Here is the code to read the C# code and output the assert statement corresponding to the comment that starts with "@@@":

```cs
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public class App
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Sample code
        string csharpCode = """
        public static int Sum(int x, int y)
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < y; i++){
                x.Inc(1);
            }
            // @@@ Value of x increases by y in the function
            return x;
        }
        """;

        FindAssertStatements(csharpCode);
        
        // Full code example
        csharpCode = @"
        // Your full code here
        ";

        FindAssertStatements(csharpCode);
    }

    public static void FindAssertStatements(string csharpCode)
    {
        string pattern = @"(\/\/\/\s*\@\@\@\s*)\n(.*?)(\n{2,})";
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(csharpCode, pattern);

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            string statement = match.Groups[2].Value.Trim();
            Console.WriteLine("<code>");
            Console.WriteLine($"Debug.Assert({statement});");
            Console.WriteLine("</code>");
        }
    }
}
```

Just replace `Your full code here` with your full code. This code will find and console the lines that match the comment pattern specified in the `FindAssertStatements` method. 

Note that this program uses C# 9.0 and later which makes it possible to match a pattern with multiple lines using `{2,}` and also we're using the new string interpolation feature. If you don't have C# 9.0 or later, you may need to adjust the code accordingly.

